"answer_core": "Do not choose a dissertation direction yet. Treat the next few weeks as a short, low-cost test rather than a commitment. The real decision turns on two gates.\n\nFirst, check whether the Silva direction can actually become a dissertation: confirm you can get early data access on a clear timeline, identify a collaborator who can compensate for the lab’s single-cell gaps, and get explicit support from your advisor rather than assuming it. Ask what data exists, who controls it, when you could inspect it, what authorship or collaboration terms would look like, and what work would still be yours.\n\nSecond, keep the fallback alive while you test Silva. It only remains a fallback if your advisor keeps it resourced, available, and technically ready for you to re-enter, and if you set a clear stop date with visible conditions for walking back. Test whether the fallback path can genuinely carry a dissertation if the Silva path fails.\n\nCoordinate with your advisor before you involve a committee. Use Dr. Silva only as a low-cost reality check on concrete data access, collaboration depth, and timing. Go to the committee only after you know whether the data path is real and whether your advisor would support the move without quietly pulling you back toward the safer lab path.\n\nRun the Silva probe strictly within the short window you define. Do dated evidence checks instead of building a finished proposal. Do not run all three options in parallel. Do not treat broad PhD success-rate claims as calibrated probabilities for this case; use them only as humility checks. Choose the path only after the Silva/data gate and the fallback gate both have concrete evidence. The best path is not the hottest story; it is the one with a real chance of becoming a finishable PhD.",
